# Building a publication document

A printed book needs one coherent document, not a sequence of callbacks over pieces
of Markdown. Before typesetting begins, the publication path must know that every
chapter can be represented, every fragment reference is valid, every destination is
unambiguous, and every contribution occupies its authored place.

The central design is to validate the whole document before materializing any
renderer-specific contribution. This turns publication preparation into a gate:
either it returns one complete structured document or it returns diagnostics.

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    Project[Parsed project] --> Order[Follow authoritative document order]
    Order --> Validate[Validate graph and every block stream]
    Validate --> Complete{Everything supported?}
    Complete -->|No| Reject[Diagnostics; no document]
    Complete -->|Yes| Materialize[Materialize trusted contribution and asset slots]
    Materialize --> Document[Complete publication document]
```

Nothing in this stage opens a process or destination. The single validation gate
collects many rules, but its consequence is simple: renderer callbacks occur only
on the accepted branch.

## Preserve the author's order

Document order is semantic. The root comes first, linked chapters follow in root
link order, and remaining chapters follow by normalized path. Fragment additions
also follow encounter order, so regrouping content by fragment family would change
the story and could change the generated program.

Each parsed document is represented as a complete sequence of blocks. A block is
either a range of the original Markdown token stream or a slot occupied by one
accepted fragment contribution. These half-open ranges and slots cover every token
exactly once. Container boundaries remain where the Markdown parser placed them,
including boundaries for nested lists and block quotations.

This representation solves a subtle problem. A contribution fence can appear inside
a list item or quotation. Splitting the source into independently parsed strings
would lose the surrounding container. Retaining the original token stream lets the
publication document replace only the contribution slot while preserving its true
structural home.

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    Tokens[One document token stream] --> Before[Markdown range]
    Tokens --> Slot[Contribution slot]
    Tokens --> After[Markdown range]
    Before --> Tree[One nested block tree]
    Slot --> Tree
    After --> Tree
```

All three regions contribute to one tree; the ranges are not reparsed separately.
Additional slots repeat the same partition without gaps or overlap.

## Stable destinations precede links

A link target should identify meaning, not appearance. Family targets derive from
exact fragment names. Contribution targets add the canonical document path and the
accepted contribution's local ordinal. Document destinations derive from canonical
paths, while heading destinations combine a document identity with an allocated
heading slug.

These identities use separate namespaces. A family, a contribution, a document, a
heading, a term, and a note therefore cannot collide merely because their visible
labels happen to match.

The component encoder can be described as follows. For a string with UTF-16 code
units `u₁` through `uₙ`, encode each unit as four hexadecimal digits and concatenate
them:

```text
E(s) = hex₄(u₁) · hex₄(u₂) · … · hex₄(uₙ)
```

Here `hex₄` means a four-digit hexadecimal representation and `·` means
concatenation. For example, `E("A") = 0041`, while `E("AB") = 00410042`. The
encoding preserves case and exact code units. Separators added between encoded
components cannot occur inside a component, so decoding boundaries are
unambiguous.

This policy gives deterministic, collision-free targets. It does not claim that all
identities survive every structural edit. Renaming a fragment changes its family
identity, and moving a contribution to another chapter changes that contribution's
identity. Such edits change what the item is in the authored structure.

Heading slugs follow a different concern: readable author links. Visible heading
text is normalized, lowercased without locale, stripped of punctuation and symbols
other than hyphens and underscores, and joined with hyphens at whitespace. Duplicate
slugs are allocated distinct destinations within their document. The enclosing
document identity prevents equal headings in different chapters from colliding.

## Validate the complete graph

Publication cannot validate only roots that happen to generate source. A book may
show and index a fragment that no root reaches. Every accepted reference in every
contribution must therefore be checked, including references in otherwise unused
families.

Validation rejects missing families, definition operators used where only a
reference is allowed, and dependency cycles. Cycle detection walks the graph
without expanding fragment text. An iterative depth-first traversal keeps an active
set for the current path and a finished set for completed families. An edge into the
active set identifies a cycle at the reference that closes it.

For `V` fragment families and `E` reference edges, this validation requires work
proportional to `V + E`. The practical point is more important than the notation:
an exponentially expanding fragment need not be expanded merely to discover that
its dependency graph is valid.

## Accept a controlled block language

The publication document is deliberately smaller than arbitrary Markdown plus
extensions. It admits the structures the print renderer understands, including
paragraphs, headings, ordinary inline formatting, links, lists, quotations,
horizontal rules, inline code, ordinary code blocks, accepted fragment
contributions, annotations, and supported standalone resources.

The ordinary parser disables raw HTML and TeX, so those author spellings remain
inert text rather than executable output. It also does not recognize pipe-table
syntax. If an unsupported raw or extension token nevertheless reaches the document
bridge, the bridge rejects it instead of guessing at a conversion. An inline image
likewise cannot be moved to a block position by guesswork. These rules protect both
meaning and safety without claiming support for structures the parser never creates.

Diagnostics use honest locations. When the Markdown parser provides only a block
line for inline children, the diagnostic points to that enclosing block instead of
inventing a character offset. Fragment references retain the more precise locations
already established by the shared scanner.

## Keep placeholders inert during validation

While the document is being checked, accepted contributions and standalone assets
remain typed placeholders. They are not magic strings inserted into prose, so they
cannot collide with author text. Containers that hold placeholders remain ordinary
pending lists, quotations, or divisions.

Only after every document and graph check succeeds are those placeholders replaced.
The caller supplies trusted functions for contribution and asset presentation. A
contribution can then become a renderer-controlled block containing syntax spans
and semantic reference links; an image can become a renderer-controlled figure
request. Successful validation permits these callbacks; it does not make arbitrary
callback behavior safe. The caller still owns the trust contract for raw renderer
output.

## The controlled Pandoc bridge

The implementation represents the completed tree using Pandoc's JSON document
shapes. This is a bridge, not an appeal to Pandoc as a second parser. Literate keeps
control of ordering, fragment recognition, reference resolution, annotations,
assets, and the supported token vocabulary. Pandoc receives already structured
data and is later used as a trusted prose-to-LaTeX writer.

`preparePandoc` names the in-memory gate. It performs no filesystem access, source
tangling, template loading, or subprocess execution. Raw inline and block nodes are
reserved for trusted renderers; the Markdown walker itself never creates them from
author markup.

The bridge also keeps metadata intentionally narrow. Publication settings and page
layout are renderer options, not unreviewed document metadata that can alter the
converter's behavior.

`publication Pandoc preparation` keeps validation ahead of trusted materialization.

```ts : <<publication Pandoc preparation>>=
export function preparePandoc(
    project: ParsedProject, renderContribution: (part: PublicationContribution) => PandocBlock[],
    renderAsset?: AssetRenderer, external?: PublicationLinkResolver
): PandocPreparationResult {
    const diagnostics = project.diagnostics.map(diagnostic => ({ ...diagnostic }));
    if (diagnostics.length) { return { diagnostics }; }
    diagnostics.push(...project.annotations.diagnostics.map(diagnostic => ({ ...diagnostic })));
    if (diagnostics.length) { return { diagnostics }; }
    validateReferences(project, diagnostics);
    const targets = new Map(project.documents.map(document => [document.path, headingDestinations(document)]));
    const parts = new Map(project.publication.contributions.map(part => [part.target, part]));
    const pending: PendingBlock[] = project.documents.map(document => {
        const contributions = contributionIndex(document, parts, diagnostics);
        const body = readBlocks({ document, index: 0, contributions, targets, diagnostics, external,
            annotations: project.annotations, publication: project.publication,
            diagrams: project.diagrams, assets: !!renderAsset });
        return { t: 'Div', c: [[targets.get(document.path)!.id, [], []], body] };
    });
    if (diagnostics.length) { return { diagnostics }; }
    const blocks = materialize(pending, renderContribution, diagnostics, renderAsset);
    if (diagnostics.length) { return { diagnostics }; }
// eslint-disable-next-line @typescript-eslint/naming-convention -- Pandoc's wire-format key.
    return { document: { 'pandoc-api-version': [1, 23, 1], meta: {}, blocks }, diagnostics };
}
```

## Complete means complete

A successful result contains one document whose blocks preserve project order and
container structure. A failed result contains diagnostics and no document. This
contract lets later stages assume that links, contributions, annotations, and
resources have already crossed a common semantic gate.

That common gate becomes especially valuable for annotations. Terms and notes need
identities that mean the same thing in screen links, print page references, and
indexes. The next chapter develops that shared annotation model.
